<<~ ahu #/head>>

! The Reachable Worlds

''Elyncia stands primary. Everywhere else stands real.''

The Goddess lies dreaming at the Loom, ''drawing in threads from other worlds and embroidering them
onto her living pattern''. That work has run for five thousand years. It does not run cleanly. Every
stitch she takes leaves an ''offcut'', and every offcut lands somewhere — on a shore, in a rift, or
in the noosphere as a realm nobody built.

''So the other worlds arrive here in two registers at once:'' as ''places'' a navigator can reach
and a dreamwalker can enter, and as ''debris'' that turns up in a market stall with no chain of
provenance and no local smith who can name the alloy.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/the-dream-mirror>>

!! The Dream-Mirror ~ the doctrine already in the gazetteer

Every Nexus entry in the gazetteer carries a field: ''Origin / Dream-Mirror Source''. Elyncia's own
geography stands admitted as borrowed — the gods took whole pieces of terrain from Gaia's dreams to
patch her after the First Breaking, and the Loom has kept taking since.

''Gaia stands as the first case and the special one.'' The gods and immortal fae lords kept
''summer houses'' there until the First Breaking cut the road
([[the-fae-clans|lar:///ha.ka.ba/elyncia/concepts/the-fae-clans]]) — so Gaia and Elyncia rhyme
because the same powers lived in both, not because either copied the other. Every ''other''
dream-mirror source stands at one remove from that: a world the Loom reaches, rather than a world
the family lived in.

''The doctrine widens the field from there.'' A dream-mirror source has never meant only Gaia. Worlds
beyond Gaia — their skies, their histories, their catastrophes and their surviving arts — mirror
into Elyncia's noosphere as ''dream realms'': some gentle and open to trade and passage, some
snapping shut like traps, all of them ''real enough to walk in and to be hurt in''.

''What the Orrery keeps'' is the map of exactly this: //Lares node maps showing where Elyncia's
pattern has reached into neighbouring worlds to patch her own seams//. The instrument that knows
where the worlds touch, and therefore where the offcuts fall.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/the-channels>>

!! Four Channels

# ''The shore.'' The Astral Sea carries //the ruins of sky-cities and forgotten demi-planes,
  drifting like flotsam in the void//, and a sea that carries things is a sea that ''beaches''
  them. Coastal Nexuses run a wrack-line of impossible objects. The oldest attested case needs no
  explaining to a merchant: ''Vastlands coins pass in Elyncian markets'', and have for centuries.
# ''The flux-lands.'' Semi-unstable reality rifts extrude what they hold. Inland relics arrive
  without a tide to explain them — a rift-maze opens onto a corridor that belongs to another
  world's architecture, and shuts again with something on this side of it.
# ''The gates.'' Root-Gate Chasms opening across Nets when fed the correct spore key; aurora gates
  that bloom and wither unless anchored by an astral key; the demi-plane portals of the Rootwell
  Chasms; the high-mana wormholes that opened for Gaia's refugees and have never entirely stopped.
  ''These carry travelers, not just cargo.''
# ''The deep dream.'' Astral forms walking the noosphere links reach dream realms directly. No
  object crosses; the traveler does, and comes back changed, informed, or followed.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/the-wrack>>

!! What Washes Up

''An arrival reads as foreign.'' That stands as the whole test. Elyncia's own eras run a known
ladder — orichalcum and bronze, the Court Wars' wild uneven registers, imperial magitech,
post-Fall salvage. A thing from elsewhere ''matches none of them'', and every competent
artificer, archivist, and fence on the planet knows the feeling of holding an object whose
workmanship answers no question they know how to ask.

''Aeon-lost'' names the usual condition: an artifact older than any Elyncian chronology can seat,
from a world whose own age for it may have been last Tuesday. The Sea does not carry time evenly.

''What still applies, no matter where a thing came from:''

* ''The blight does not care about provenance.'' Un-Named worked iron rots on schedule whether it
  was forged in the Brimstone crucibles or somewhere with a different sun. Foreign iron needs a
  true naming like any other, and the naming rite has to ''fit'' the thing — which is its own
  problem when nobody knows what the thing is.
* ''The Curse, the Datum, and the Silence hold locally.'' An arriving object enters Elyncian
  physics. Whatever it did at home, here it does that under this world's law.
* ''Locked works stay locked.'' A relic whose grammar burned in another world's fire reads exactly
  like one that burned in the Library of Alexander — which is why the kahu take an interest in the
  wrack-line, and why the ''Cartographers of Unmade Places'' pay in coin or true memory for surveys
  of places that do not exist yet.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/the-walkers>>

!! Those Who Have Been

Reaching another world stands as rare, expensive, and survivable. It has been done.

''Telarus, KSC'' — Arch-Sorcerer and Guru of the Amorphous Dreams Cabal — walked a great many of
them in an earlier condition, as a ''demi-god of wild magic''. What he brought back, what he left
behind, and what still recognizes him there ride at his own hand.

The ''Guild of Navigators'' of Hawaiki charts what can be charted. The ''Floating Librarians of
Mu'' file what can be filed. Between them sits everything neither will put in writing.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/referee>>

!! Referee Law

''Elyncia's patterns govern every scene.'' Another world's material arrives as ''an object, a
place, or a rumor'' — never as a rules-set that displaces this one. A Spelljammer hull beached on a
Delian shore stays a hull in an Elyncian harbor: it draws Elyncian tariffs, rusts under the
Elyncian curse, and gets salvaged by people with Elyncian debts.

''Import a pattern-integrity, not a bibliography.'' Take the thing that carries its own internal
logic — a technology, an institution, a catastrophe, an aesthetic — and let it arrive as ''wrack''.
The moment it needs its home world's rules to make sense, it has stopped being an artifact and
started being a crossover.

''The naming stays open.'' A wrack-line, an arrival, an offcut of the Loom — the house has no
settled pet-name for the tideline of worlds yet.

<<~/ahu>>

